Subject: QTL very close to each other and %Var explained
Message-ID: <bX38P-slUdU.K9Vcl61gAgAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/bX38P-slUdU/m/K9Vcl61gAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

In my multiple QTL scan for an F2 population, I got two pairs of QTL about 1 cM apart on the same linkage group. From reading previous posts, I went to check if there are outlier individuals at these markers that led to these closely located peaks. In one case, it is easy to identity an outlier individual for genotype AA that is present for marker at 26.5 but not for marker at 27(see chr5pos26.5 and chr5pos27). After excluding this one individual, the QTL at 26.5 dropped out.

For another pair of peaks, however, it is not so easy to pick out any outlier that is present at one marker but not the other. The phenotypic effect is much smaller and not so clean to begin with. Both had LOD score around 7 from drop one function in a multiple QTL model. The phenotypic distribution for the three genotypes are very similar at both markers (see chr5pos0 and chr5pos1), and both slightly skewed in the same way. Do you have any suggestion on what may have caused this pair of QTL to be so close?

My second question regards %var explained in a multiple QTL model. I found that the %var explained of the full model (with no interaction terms) is not the sum of the %var explained by each marker from the drop one QTL at a time ANOVA table. Typically it is greater than the sum. I wonder what is the explanation for this discrepancy, and what is your suggestion if we want to report both %var explained by each marker, and %var of the full model.

Many thanks!

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Tue Aug 29 14:35:13 2017
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Tue, 29 Aug 2017 14:35:13 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl-disc] switch.order abuse?
Message-ID: <hQPO-m4vv4o.W88gEK18AgAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <hQPO-m4vv4o.QITN1IvxCAAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <hQPO-m4vv4o.W8R7zAAIAQAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com> <hQPO-m4vv4o.QITN1IvxCAAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/hQPO-m4vv4o/m/W88gEK18AgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If you ensure that the two crosses have the same markers in the same order, you could use ripple() on a given chromosome with each cross, and then sum either the LOD scores of the crossover counts.

  rip1 <- ripple(cross1, method="countxo", chr=3, window=7)
  rip2 <- ripple(cross2, method="countxo", chr=3, window=7)
  rip <- rip1
  rip[,"obligXO"] <- rip1[,"obligXO"] + rip2[,"obligXO"]
  summary(rip)

or for the likelihood method:

  rip1 <- ripple(cross1, method="likelihood", chr=3, window=4)
  rip2 <- ripple(cross2, method="likelihood", chr=3, window=4)
  rip <- rip1
  rip[,"LOD"] <- rip1[,"LOD"] + rip2[,"LOD"]
  summary(rip)

Alternatively, if you're willing to assume that the recombination rates are the same in the two crosses, you could combine your two 4-way cross objects into one and build the map using the two crosses combined.

karl

